1
00:00:11,261 --> 00:00:12,428
[knocks on door]

2
00:00:12,429 --> 00:00:13,514
{\an8}Shit.

3
00:00:16,391 --> 00:00:17,601
[blows]

4
00:00:28,278 --> 00:00:30,613
- Hey. Finally on my way home.
- Hi.

5
00:00:30,614 --> 00:00:32,322
Just wanted to check in.

6
00:00:32,323 --> 00:00:34,743
- Big day today.
- Yeah, it was.

7
00:00:35,494 --> 00:00:37,579
- All good?
- Yeah.

8
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:39,915
You did well at the Council.

9
00:00:40,582 --> 00:00:42,668
I think it's the start
of something special.

10
00:00:44,336 --> 00:00:46,255
It smells like smoke in here.

11
00:00:49,174 --> 00:00:54,555
Oh. Oh, I... I didn't like the soup,
so I, uh, reheated some pizza.

12
00:00:56,515 --> 00:00:57,807
Okay, well...

13
00:00:57,808 --> 00:00:59,309
- Night.
- Night.

14
00:01:05,022 --> 00:01:06,358
[sighs]

15
00:01:16,451 --> 00:01:19,288
[The Algorithm] <i>The note itself
is not of grave concern.</i>

16
00:01:20,163 --> 00:01:24,042
<i>What is of concern is her decision
to lie to you about it.</i>

17
00:01:25,419 --> 00:01:28,255
<i>The medication can only erase what was,</i>

18
00:01:29,089 --> 00:01:31,799
<i>but if she does not trust
the new narrative,</i>

19
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:33,594
<i>we have a very different problem.</i>

20
00:01:34,386 --> 00:01:38,015
<i>And you, Camille,
are the narrator she needs to trust.</i>

21
00:01:38,557 --> 00:01:41,643
I don't understand
why this is taking so long.

22
00:01:42,519 --> 00:01:45,105
You said a month, two at most.

23
00:01:45,856 --> 00:01:48,441
[The Algorithm] <i>We have 352 years of data</i>

24
00:01:48,442 --> 00:01:51,736
<i>which demonstrates
the protocol is effective.</i>

25
00:01:51,737 --> 00:01:53,196
<i>Most of the time.</i>

26
00:01:53,197 --> 00:01:55,032
<i>When implemented correctly.</i>

27
00:01:55,824 --> 00:02:02,455
<i>Six silos have successfully undergone full
resets, including your own 140 years ago.</i>

28
00:02:02,456 --> 00:02:05,834
<i>And there have been
individuals targeted along the way.</i>

29
00:02:08,294 --> 00:02:09,880
<i>Your frustration is noted.</i>

30
00:02:11,256 --> 00:02:13,257
<i>While it has largely proven effective,</i>

31
00:02:13,258 --> 00:02:15,384
<i>there have also been exceptions.</i>

32
00:02:15,385 --> 00:02:19,639
<i>Outliers like Juliette Nichols who
have required extended treatment.</i>

33
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:22,767
<i>You, Camille,
are working with such an outlier.</i>

34
00:02:22,768 --> 00:02:24,770
I feel so much better now.

35
00:02:25,938 --> 00:02:28,397
[The Algorithm] <i>Her dream indicates
some memory of her return</i>

36
00:02:28,398 --> 00:02:29,900
<i>remains in her unconscious.</i>

37
00:02:31,235 --> 00:02:34,071
<i>Continue the medication protocol
for two more weeks.</i>

38
00:02:34,696 --> 00:02:37,990
<i>Tighten her security detail
and double your efforts</i>

39
00:02:37,991 --> 00:02:41,870
<i>to assure Nichols that you are
her most trusted ally.</i>

40
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:45,832
[Daniel] Thirty miles out of Cartersville.

41
00:02:46,458 --> 00:02:49,503
Kick-ass view of the sunset
over Pine Mountain.

42
00:02:50,796 --> 00:02:56,301
Yeah, we had, um,
horses and ducks and goats.

43
00:02:59,054 --> 00:03:00,430
These two bulldogs.

44
00:03:02,307 --> 00:03:04,433
Angriest dogs God put on the earth.

45
00:03:04,434 --> 00:03:07,395
Only thing they ever did was
chase us from the front gate

46
00:03:07,396 --> 00:03:09,356
to the house every
time we got off the bus.

47
00:03:11,859 --> 00:03:12,860
[chuckles]

48
00:03:13,443 --> 00:03:14,443
[sniffles]

49
00:03:14,444 --> 00:03:19,783
When I told you I-I'd been made the, uh,
University of Georgia Bulldogs' mascot...

50
00:03:23,245 --> 00:03:24,245
Uh, don't...

51
00:03:24,246 --> 00:03:25,330
Don't worry.

52
00:03:26,081 --> 00:03:27,415
What?

53
00:03:27,416 --> 00:03:29,877
Uh, well, you laughed so hard that you...

54
00:03:31,753 --> 00:03:33,379
you pissed yourself. [chuckles]

55
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:37,884
[chuckles] I don't sound
like a very nice sister.

56
00:03:37,885 --> 00:03:39,094
[sighs]

57
00:03:41,138 --> 00:03:45,893
Mom's name was Grace.

58
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:47,603
Dad...

59
00:03:49,354 --> 00:03:50,397
William.

60
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:53,065
Please, I'm...

61
00:03:53,066 --> 00:03:56,195
I-I'm sorry, Donald, I just...

62
00:03:56,695 --> 00:03:57,945
don't know what to say.

63
00:03:57,946 --> 00:04:00,032
No. Um... Um...

64
00:04:02,743 --> 00:04:05,578
- Um, no. Uh, don't be sorry. I'm sorry.
- Um...

65
00:04:05,579 --> 00:04:09,416
- [intercom beeps]
- [announcement, indistinct]

66
00:04:22,846 --> 00:04:23,889
[beeps]

67
00:04:28,852 --> 00:04:30,729
[grunts] Fuck.

68
00:04:32,147 --> 00:04:33,773
- [grunts]
- [doctor] You all right?

69
00:04:33,774 --> 00:04:36,067
I don't usually swear
at inanimate objects.

70
00:04:36,068 --> 00:04:38,361
[doctor] Ah, worse targets for rage.

71
00:04:38,362 --> 00:04:40,989
Besides, this machine's
been asking for it for months.

72
00:04:42,199 --> 00:04:44,575
Mmm. [chews] That's good.

73
00:04:44,576 --> 00:04:46,370
Don't worry about that.

74
00:04:47,079 --> 00:04:48,413
Everything okay?

75
00:04:49,665 --> 00:04:51,291
Yeah. My sister's here.

76
00:04:52,709 --> 00:04:54,044
Well, her body's here.

77
00:04:54,753 --> 00:04:57,588
I'm sitting there with someone
I've spoken to every day for the last

78
00:04:57,589 --> 00:05:01,093
three decades and she's looking
at me like I'm the Uber driver.

79
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:02,760
You're Daniel.

80
00:05:02,761 --> 00:05:05,639
We've been expecting you.
I'm Victor Crnkovich.

81
00:05:06,265 --> 00:05:08,182
- I'm Charlotte's doctor.
- Oh.

82
00:05:08,183 --> 00:05:09,851
- Hi.
- Um, hi.

83
00:05:09,852 --> 00:05:11,227
It's good to meet you.

84
00:05:11,228 --> 00:05:14,064
Yeah, I'm afraid to tell you that I'm
the reason she doesn't remember you.

85
00:07:11,265 --> 00:07:13,058
[Victor] So, the medication,

86
00:07:13,725 --> 00:07:17,270
i-it's like installing a drawbridge
between her and her memory.

87
00:07:17,271 --> 00:07:20,690
A safety gap, if you will,
between upstairs and downstairs.

88
00:07:20,691 --> 00:07:24,110
Used for too long, the bridge stays up,
becomes permanent

89
00:07:24,111 --> 00:07:25,611
and she never gets her memory back.

90
00:07:25,612 --> 00:07:29,365
But when she's stronger,
we'll pause the medication,

91
00:07:29,366 --> 00:07:32,076
lower the bridge, go across, and choose...

92
00:07:32,077 --> 00:07:33,619
actually choose...

93
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:36,123
what memories to bring back,
and what to leave behind.

94
00:07:40,794 --> 00:07:42,004
[sighs]

95
00:07:45,507 --> 00:07:48,801
I lost you with the drawbridge thing?

96
00:07:48,802 --> 00:07:49,887
No.

97
00:07:51,138 --> 00:07:52,806
I-I don't know. Yes, uh...

98
00:07:53,599 --> 00:07:55,558
This kind of conversation
wa-wasn't on my list

99
00:07:55,559 --> 00:07:57,311
of things to try to get
my head around today.

100
00:07:58,395 --> 00:08:01,814
The drawbridge comes down,
how do you get her memories back?

101
00:08:01,815 --> 00:08:03,901
We tell her story, over and over.

102
00:08:04,526 --> 00:08:05,652
Her story?

103
00:08:06,445 --> 00:08:07,446
Okay.

104
00:08:08,405 --> 00:08:14,244
[grunts] Why do humans
rule the world and not-not the apes?

105
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:18,080
I don't know.

106
00:08:18,081 --> 00:08:19,416
Opposable thumb.

107
00:08:21,084 --> 00:08:22,211
Better motor control.

108
00:08:22,794 --> 00:08:24,962
- Bigger brain. Less hair.
- Okay.

109
00:08:24,963 --> 00:08:27,507
Let's try this. What do you do all day?

110
00:08:27,508 --> 00:08:30,885
Right now? Uh, call up total strangers
and beg them for money.

111
00:08:30,886 --> 00:08:34,472
Exactly. You reach out to people
and you tell them a story.

112
00:08:35,182 --> 00:08:37,725
You take a set of data
and you weave meaning around it.

113
00:08:37,726 --> 00:08:41,019
It's what we do, it's who we are.
We're storytellers, meaning-makers.

114
00:08:41,020 --> 00:08:42,355
It's our only superpower.

115
00:08:42,356 --> 00:08:43,940
You, me, Charlotte,

116
00:08:43,941 --> 00:08:46,943
we're really just a collection of stories
we've told ourselves.

117
00:08:46,944 --> 00:08:49,070
Memory is our internal biography.

118
00:08:49,071 --> 00:08:52,908
Information that we've hopped up
with meaning to tell us who we are.

119
00:08:54,201 --> 00:08:57,204
The medication has
erased the book of Charlotte.

120
00:08:58,372 --> 00:09:00,206
Okay? Like I said,

121
00:09:00,207 --> 00:09:03,000
we'll stop the meds, and memory by memory,

122
00:09:03,001 --> 00:09:05,503
you'll fill it back up
again with her story.

123
00:09:05,504 --> 00:09:06,796
Memory by memory?

124
00:09:06,797 --> 00:09:07,880
Memory by memory.

125
00:09:07,881 --> 00:09:09,174
Loving sister,

126
00:09:09,925 --> 00:09:11,593
brilliant naval aviator.

127
00:09:12,094 --> 00:09:14,929
Even the mission to Iran,
but this time you have a choice.

128
00:09:14,930 --> 00:09:18,808
Daniel, you have a choice not to
cripple her with the whole truth.

129
00:09:18,809 --> 00:09:21,310
The trauma of what happened
to her squadron.

130
00:09:21,311 --> 00:09:22,980
You have a choice to leave that behind.

131
00:09:32,698 --> 00:09:33,991
Could I tell her a lie?

132
00:09:34,783 --> 00:09:35,784
Would she believe it?

133
00:09:37,369 --> 00:09:38,786
Why would you lie?

134
00:09:38,787 --> 00:09:41,497
Well, she has a few opinions about me
I wouldn't mind her forgetting.

135
00:09:41,498 --> 00:09:42,748
Oh.

136
00:09:42,749 --> 00:09:45,418
[chuckles]

137
00:09:45,419 --> 00:09:48,296
Actually, you-you could implant a lie.

138
00:09:48,297 --> 00:09:51,465
It would take a lot of time
and great effort.

139
00:09:51,466 --> 00:09:54,093
The real memories ignite faster
because they're already there on the page,

140
00:09:54,094 --> 00:09:55,512
she just can't see them.

141
00:09:56,096 --> 00:09:57,139
[sighs]

142
00:10:01,351 --> 00:10:05,104
You'll need artifacts
to bring back the old memories.

143
00:10:05,105 --> 00:10:08,775
You know, objects from her life,
her childhood.

144
00:10:09,610 --> 00:10:13,654
Memory is in the body, not just the brain.

145
00:10:13,655 --> 00:10:14,989
It's, uh...

146
00:10:14,990 --> 00:10:17,450
It's in the smells of the past,
and the-the meaning

147
00:10:17,451 --> 00:10:19,827
that we wrapped around objects
we had when we were kids.

148
00:10:19,828 --> 00:10:23,624
We use them like shortcuts
to ignite the neural pathways.

149
00:10:25,501 --> 00:10:27,377
Our parents died when we were young.

150
00:10:28,921 --> 00:10:30,421
We moved in with our aunt.

151
00:10:30,422 --> 00:10:32,549
There's not a lot of stuff
from our childhood.

152
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:38,304
I'm sorry, but there's something else
there between the ones and the zeros.

153
00:10:38,305 --> 00:10:41,182
It's-It's not just neurons and axons,
there's something else.

154
00:10:41,183 --> 00:10:44,228
Something mysterious, like...

155
00:10:44,978 --> 00:10:46,104
like a soul.

156
00:10:46,939 --> 00:10:49,775
And it won't be
controlled by me or anyone else.

157
00:10:51,193 --> 00:10:56,865
So, sometimes, maybe 10% of the time,
it does not work.

158
00:10:58,200 --> 00:11:03,121
But she's responding well,
so you have reason to hope.

159
00:11:04,665 --> 00:11:07,209
Okay? Okay.

160
00:11:16,510 --> 00:11:18,554
- [knocking on door]
- [door opens]

161
00:11:19,429 --> 00:11:22,431
Sorry for the interruption, boss...
[sighs] ...There's a woman out here.

162
00:11:22,432 --> 00:11:24,225
Filed a regular missing persons' report,

163
00:11:24,226 --> 00:11:26,018
but she won't leave till she sees you.

164
00:11:26,019 --> 00:11:28,981
It's not a regular
missing persons' report.

165
00:11:36,446 --> 00:11:37,864
The missing person's name?

166
00:11:37,865 --> 00:11:39,533
Orla Kent.

167
00:11:40,242 --> 00:11:41,368
She's my sister.

168
00:11:41,869 --> 00:11:43,370
Shadow to the head of Supply?

169
00:11:45,038 --> 00:11:47,915
I saw her yesterday at the Silo Council.

170
00:11:47,916 --> 00:11:49,126
Spoke to her.

171
00:11:51,587 --> 00:11:53,546
- She's missing?
- She comes to me every night

172
00:11:53,547 --> 00:11:54,630
after her shift.

173
00:11:54,631 --> 00:11:57,133
Last night, nothing.

174
00:11:57,134 --> 00:11:59,093
Maybe she went home and crashed.

175
00:11:59,094 --> 00:12:00,596
No, she'd porter a note.

176
00:12:01,221 --> 00:12:02,388
My parents are old.

177
00:12:02,389 --> 00:12:05,224
They worry. That's our deal.

178
00:12:05,225 --> 00:12:06,350
Since we were kids.

179
00:12:06,351 --> 00:12:09,021
No unexplained absences, no room to worry.

180
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:14,150
Look, I know you're busy
with a whole lot of stuff

181
00:12:14,151 --> 00:12:17,446
that's more important to everyone
else in the Silo, but something's wrong.

182
00:12:18,197 --> 00:12:19,990
I know it in my bones.

183
00:12:22,451 --> 00:12:23,702
I lied to my parents.

184
00:12:24,536 --> 00:12:26,788
I told them
Orla stayed with me last night.

185
00:12:28,248 --> 00:12:30,250
I can't lie to them again today.

186
00:12:33,295 --> 00:12:35,255
Okay. Uh...

187
00:12:37,007 --> 00:12:38,300
Okay.

188
00:12:46,808 --> 00:12:48,101
Thank you.

189
00:12:57,736 --> 00:12:59,904
[music playing on speaker]

190
00:12:59,905 --> 00:13:01,532
It's all very Frankenstein.

191
00:13:02,616 --> 00:13:03,991
[Anna] Crnkovich?

192
00:13:03,992 --> 00:13:05,535
No, uh, me.

193
00:13:05,536 --> 00:13:06,869
He's asking me to...

194
00:13:06,870 --> 00:13:09,830
[stammers] ...I don't know,
take a shopping cart into her brain,

195
00:13:09,831 --> 00:13:12,626
pick a whole lot of parts,
rebuild her soul.

196
00:13:14,378 --> 00:13:15,462
Well...

197
00:13:17,673 --> 00:13:21,343
I mean, wh-who are we
if not the sum of our memories, right?

198
00:13:23,637 --> 00:13:24,805
I guess.

199
00:13:25,806 --> 00:13:29,142
Look, I thought I might go
check on her if that's okay.

200
00:13:29,977 --> 00:13:33,229
I never served, so, you know, some candy,
some magazines to a wounded vet

201
00:13:33,230 --> 00:13:35,022
feels like literally the least I could do.

202
00:13:35,023 --> 00:13:36,233
Sure.

203
00:13:41,071 --> 00:13:42,155
Daniel.

204
00:13:43,615 --> 00:13:45,617
- Hey.
- Helen.

205
00:13:46,410 --> 00:13:47,910
- Hi.
- Hi. [chuckles]

206
00:13:47,911 --> 00:13:50,454
Uh, have you met, uh, Anna Thurman?

207
00:13:50,455 --> 00:13:51,831
Helen Drew.

208
00:13:51,832 --> 00:13:53,208
Not officially, um...

209
00:13:54,042 --> 00:13:56,127
Everyone says your mother's talking points
are bulletproof

210
00:13:56,128 --> 00:13:58,171
since she roped you in, so...

211
00:13:58,172 --> 00:14:01,340
Helen and I
accidentally ended up on a date.

212
00:14:01,341 --> 00:14:03,259
Ooh. [chuckles] Not a date.

213
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:04,344
Um...

214
00:14:04,970 --> 00:14:07,513
Listen, I just went by your office.
I heard about your sister.

215
00:14:07,514 --> 00:14:10,725
- I wanted to say sorry.
- Thanks.

216
00:14:10,726 --> 00:14:12,268
Is she at Walter Reed?

217
00:14:12,269 --> 00:14:15,689
No, they, uh, um... transferred her
to the Heidi Stensen Clinic.

218
00:14:16,315 --> 00:14:17,316
Wow.

219
00:14:18,025 --> 00:14:19,817
Someone must have
pulled some serious strings.

220
00:14:19,818 --> 00:14:23,571
I can't imagine a naval aviator would
have that kind of money tucked away.

221
00:14:23,572 --> 00:14:25,698
Actually,
I think you'll find that Dr. Crnkovich

222
00:14:25,699 --> 00:14:29,036
has been treating combat veterans
free of charge for years now.

223
00:14:29,828 --> 00:14:33,039
Um... [chuckles] Yeah, I guess. Yeah.

224
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:35,458
His first trial was on prison populations

225
00:14:35,459 --> 00:14:39,003
in countries too indebted
to US aid to say no.

226
00:14:39,004 --> 00:14:41,172
His second was with the DOD
on first year recruits

227
00:14:41,173 --> 00:14:43,549
who don't get a say
in their medical treatment.

228
00:14:43,550 --> 00:14:47,053
And in both cases,
he took a big blackboard eraser

229
00:14:47,054 --> 00:14:48,763
and wiped out their memories.

230
00:14:48,764 --> 00:14:52,642
He let traumatized combat vets
forget the horrors that they saw.

231
00:14:52,643 --> 00:14:55,102
He let career criminals forget
that they were criminals

232
00:14:55,103 --> 00:14:56,812
and become productive members of society.

233
00:14:56,813 --> 00:15:00,275
But he decides what people remember
and what they don't remember.

234
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:02,361
Doesn't that worry you?

235
00:15:03,779 --> 00:15:07,157
Well, it's a little more complicated
than that.

236
00:15:07,866 --> 00:15:08,866
And she's my sister.

237
00:15:08,867 --> 00:15:12,412
You know, everything since she became
a fighter pilot worries me.

238
00:15:12,913 --> 00:15:16,541
Is it too cynical to say that he is
using injured vets to test his theories?

239
00:15:16,542 --> 00:15:18,543
- Helen.
- Victor lost his wife

240
00:15:18,544 --> 00:15:21,296
and child in a car accident
when he fell asleep at the wheel.

241
00:15:23,090 --> 00:15:25,300
My guess is he's got more skin
in the trauma game than most.

242
00:15:27,553 --> 00:15:28,554
Hmm.

243
00:15:30,806 --> 00:15:31,806
Take care.

244
00:15:31,807 --> 00:15:34,600
And, uh, send Charlotte my best.

245
00:15:34,601 --> 00:15:35,686
Sure.

246
00:15:42,109 --> 00:15:44,945
You met with a reporter
and you thought it was a date?

247
00:15:46,655 --> 00:15:49,448
Politicians and reporters,
you know it's transact--

248
00:15:49,449 --> 00:15:51,826
It's transactional, yes, I know.

249
00:15:51,827 --> 00:15:53,744
[stammers] I was, uh...

250
00:15:53,745 --> 00:15:55,205
Distracted?

251
00:15:57,082 --> 00:16:01,169
Well, as well as being quite good looking,
she's also good at her job.

252
00:16:01,170 --> 00:16:03,671
Even if the only one she could get
was a shitty one.

253
00:16:03,672 --> 00:16:05,923
- Is she not at the <i>Post</i>?
- Mm-mmm.

254
00:16:05,924 --> 00:16:07,759
No, she hasn't been there for a year.

255
00:16:08,260 --> 00:16:09,553
She was a bad girl.

256
00:16:10,179 --> 00:16:13,557
She's at some online sleaze factory now.

257
00:16:14,391 --> 00:16:16,976
Still, she's a reporter.

258
00:16:16,977 --> 00:16:18,645
She got what she wanted
and you got nothing.

259
00:16:19,688 --> 00:16:20,689
What'd she get?

260
00:16:21,857 --> 00:16:23,942
You told her where
Charlotte's being treated.

261
00:16:26,028 --> 00:16:27,529
And you told her for free.

262
00:16:38,624 --> 00:16:39,625
[clicks tongue]

263
00:16:52,721 --> 00:16:54,389
She said you should take the whole box...

264
00:16:55,390 --> 00:16:56,391
for luck.

265
00:16:58,143 --> 00:16:59,561
Okay?

266
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:03,231
{\an8}[Martha] <i>"You wanted the truth.</i>

267
00:17:03,232 --> 00:17:05,442
{\an8}<i>The truth is I love you.</i>

268
00:17:06,359 --> 00:17:10,531
<i>Have no fear, they're good in Supply."</i>

269
00:17:16,244 --> 00:17:17,496
Mayor?

270
00:17:18,664 --> 00:17:19,665
Mayor?

271
00:17:20,415 --> 00:17:23,376
Mrs. Sims asked me to log
our movements more formally today.

272
00:17:23,377 --> 00:17:24,585
[tools whirring]

273
00:17:24,586 --> 00:17:25,877
[Shirley] Get back to Supply.

274
00:17:25,878 --> 00:17:29,091
If they don't find us more rebar,
the stairs...

275
00:17:30,175 --> 00:17:32,469
they'll only be ready
for their grandchildren.

276
00:17:35,222 --> 00:17:36,223
Cover me.

277
00:17:45,566 --> 00:17:47,651
You know, you still gotta talk to her.

278
00:17:48,151 --> 00:17:49,152
I can't.

279
00:17:59,037 --> 00:18:00,539
She asked for your help.

280
00:18:05,544 --> 00:18:06,544
I can't.

281
00:18:06,545 --> 00:18:07,629
Okay.

282
00:18:08,172 --> 00:18:09,672
Maybe I should check with someone.

283
00:18:09,673 --> 00:18:12,176
[stammers] It's safe and all
to go down but...

284
00:18:12,676 --> 00:18:14,385
Why? Who would you check with?

285
00:18:14,386 --> 00:18:17,305
I'm the mayor. This is my security.

286
00:18:17,306 --> 00:18:18,640
[Knox] Teddy.

287
00:18:19,558 --> 00:18:20,933
What's going on?

288
00:18:20,934 --> 00:18:23,979
Uh, the mayor wants to go down.

289
00:18:24,980 --> 00:18:26,231
Oh.

290
00:18:26,940 --> 00:18:28,859
Madam Mayor,
can I ask why you want to go down?

291
00:18:30,027 --> 00:18:31,570
Do I have to give you a reason?

292
00:18:32,070 --> 00:18:35,198
Well, I'm in charge
of rebuilding the stairs

293
00:18:35,199 --> 00:18:37,326
and no one gets on a rope
without my say-so.

294
00:18:41,788 --> 00:18:46,000
Um, you know I don't really remember you?

295
00:18:46,001 --> 00:18:47,169
Yeah, I know.

296
00:18:47,920 --> 00:18:51,006
And I have this feeling
we didn't really see eye to eye.

297
00:18:51,673 --> 00:18:52,883
We did all right.

298
00:18:55,135 --> 00:18:58,304
- Uh, I wanna talk to Martha Walker.
- Oh.

299
00:18:58,305 --> 00:19:01,350
Well, you're in luck. She went up to 60
to get some medical supplies.

300
00:19:02,226 --> 00:19:03,936
She should be back down any second.

301
00:19:05,896 --> 00:19:06,897
Right.

302
00:19:07,773 --> 00:19:09,775
Well, we, um... we'll wait.

303
00:19:11,443 --> 00:19:13,487
Uh, Madam Mayor,

304
00:19:14,613 --> 00:19:16,490
while you wait,
can I talk to you for a second?

305
00:19:17,741 --> 00:19:18,742
In private.

306
00:19:19,493 --> 00:19:20,994
Yeah. Sure.

307
00:19:23,956 --> 00:19:24,957
It's okay.

308
00:19:28,877 --> 00:19:31,505
I, uh... Shit. [chuckles]

309
00:19:32,881 --> 00:19:36,843
Camille gave us all instructions
about how to talk to you

310
00:19:36,844 --> 00:19:38,219
so I don't fuck up your recovery.

311
00:19:38,220 --> 00:19:40,013
Oh, it's okay. Go ahead.

312
00:19:43,767 --> 00:19:45,769
You asked Shirley about Bernard Holland.

313
00:19:46,436 --> 00:19:47,436
Yeah?

314
00:19:47,437 --> 00:19:49,147
This morning she remembered something.

315
00:19:49,940 --> 00:19:54,528
I don't know if this is helpful,
but during the rebellion... [sighs]

316
00:19:55,279 --> 00:19:56,613
...a guy came down.

317
00:19:57,656 --> 00:19:59,116
He said he was Bernard's shadow.

318
00:20:00,492 --> 00:20:03,619
Well, we didn't have time for him,
Shirl was just gonna throw him in a cell,

319
00:20:03,620 --> 00:20:05,330
but he said he knew you.

320
00:20:06,456 --> 00:20:08,416
He said that when you went over that hill,

321
00:20:08,417 --> 00:20:10,793
someone had seen you go inside somewhere

322
00:20:10,794 --> 00:20:13,630
and that maybe you were still alive.

323
00:20:14,298 --> 00:20:16,216
- Did he say who?
- No.

324
00:20:17,301 --> 00:20:19,553
All I know is Shirl took him down
to the digger void.

325
00:20:20,596 --> 00:20:22,096
He was looking for something
at the bottom.

326
00:20:22,097 --> 00:20:23,556
He wouldn't say what it was.

327
00:20:23,557 --> 00:20:25,851
But if this guy really was
Bernard's shadow,

328
00:20:27,060 --> 00:20:28,228
maybe he could help you.

329
00:20:29,646 --> 00:20:31,064
[stammers] What's his name?

330
00:20:32,232 --> 00:20:33,609
Lukas Kyle.

331
00:20:38,780 --> 00:20:42,241
[sighs] I'm getting an assistant
in my next life.

332
00:20:42,242 --> 00:20:46,704
I am so done with doing things for someone
who should be doing them himself.

333
00:20:46,705 --> 00:20:48,789
Hi. Let me start over.

334
00:20:48,790 --> 00:20:51,375
I'm with Congressman
Daniel Keene's office.

335
00:20:51,376 --> 00:20:53,503
He asked me to stop by,
check on his sister.

336
00:20:53,504 --> 00:20:55,338
Maybe, um... [smacks lips]

337
00:20:55,339 --> 00:20:57,174
...maybe get her outside?

338
00:20:59,259 --> 00:21:01,386
Why didn't Shirley tell me herself?

339
00:21:05,349 --> 00:21:06,475
I think...

340
00:21:08,227 --> 00:21:09,353
[sighs]

341
00:21:10,854 --> 00:21:13,564
We're all struggling
with how to wrap our heads around

342
00:21:13,565 --> 00:21:15,816
what you remember and what you don't.

343
00:21:15,817 --> 00:21:16,943
[grunts]

344
00:21:16,944 --> 00:21:20,239
[chuckles] And I know... I know
that you won't remember this,

345
00:21:21,740 --> 00:21:25,244
but Shirley,
she armors up when she's hurting.

346
00:21:28,872 --> 00:21:31,375
I mean, when you first came back,

347
00:21:32,793 --> 00:21:36,921
Shirl camped outside your hospital room
for ten days straight.

348
00:21:36,922 --> 00:21:38,257
Wouldn't leave your side.

349
00:21:39,132 --> 00:21:42,219
I practically had to carry her
back down to Mechanical.

350
00:21:44,096 --> 00:21:47,932
Then the rumors started that you had lost
a lot of your memory in the fire,

351
00:21:47,933 --> 00:21:50,059
but Shirl wouldn't have it.

352
00:21:50,060 --> 00:21:51,562
You wouldn't forget her.

353
00:21:52,145 --> 00:21:57,400
She ran up 144 levels to be there
your first time out in the cafeteria.

354
00:21:57,401 --> 00:21:58,777
Waited in the back.

355
00:21:59,278 --> 00:22:01,572
Let everybody else get their piece of you.

356
00:22:03,156 --> 00:22:06,243
And then when it was her turn,
you looked straight through her.

357
00:22:09,413 --> 00:22:10,414
I...

358
00:22:12,207 --> 00:22:17,087
I just think talking to you
hurts a little too much right now.

359
00:22:21,592 --> 00:22:22,718
Here's Walker.

360
00:22:26,847 --> 00:22:30,434
Madam Mayor, this is Martha Walker.

361
00:22:31,226 --> 00:22:32,351
Don't bother.

362
00:22:32,352 --> 00:22:34,645
She has no idea who I am.

363
00:22:34,646 --> 00:22:36,064
Do you?

364
00:22:38,275 --> 00:22:39,483
Let's start with something simple.

365
00:22:39,484 --> 00:22:40,902
You still drink coffee?

366
00:22:40,903 --> 00:22:45,115
Or am I not allowed to ask that
'cause you're too fragile?

367
00:22:46,241 --> 00:22:47,326
I drink coffee.

368
00:22:49,745 --> 00:22:50,829
Good.

369
00:22:56,043 --> 00:22:57,793
So what have they been telling you?

370
00:22:57,794 --> 00:23:00,171
[huffs] Well, they said that I used to

371
00:23:00,172 --> 00:23:02,798
- spend a lot of time in your workshop.
- [resident 1] The mayor's coming.

372
00:23:02,799 --> 00:23:04,842
[resident 2] Madam Mayor, go ahead.

373
00:23:04,843 --> 00:23:07,054
She can wait like the rest of us.

374
00:23:11,642 --> 00:23:12,643
So...

375
00:23:13,310 --> 00:23:18,273
I know that my main responsibility
was the generator, right?

376
00:23:19,358 --> 00:23:22,152
But I wanna know why...
why did I spend so much time with you?

377
00:23:23,654 --> 00:23:25,404
You came for advice.

378
00:23:25,405 --> 00:23:26,740
Which you never took.

379
00:23:27,699 --> 00:23:28,742
Why?

380
00:23:29,535 --> 00:23:31,285
- Hi.
- Pigheadedness, arrogance.

381
00:23:31,286 --> 00:23:33,829
Hey, two black coffees.

382
00:23:33,830 --> 00:23:34,915
Hold the fawning.

383
00:23:38,961 --> 00:23:40,671
So why'd you wanna see me?

384
00:23:43,340 --> 00:23:45,342
I got a note last night.

385
00:23:46,760 --> 00:23:48,177
Did you send it?

386
00:23:48,178 --> 00:23:49,387
Wasn't me.

387
00:23:49,388 --> 00:23:50,722
What did it say?

388
00:23:51,849 --> 00:23:52,932
Oh.

389
00:23:52,933 --> 00:23:54,309
You don't trust me.

390
00:23:55,769 --> 00:23:56,936
I want to trust you.

391
00:23:56,937 --> 00:23:58,396
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

392
00:23:58,397 --> 00:24:00,190
W-Why would that be?

393
00:24:03,360 --> 00:24:05,946
I remember you sent me a note
the day that I went out to clean.

394
00:24:08,740 --> 00:24:10,284
[whispering] "They're good in Supply."

395
00:24:10,826 --> 00:24:13,203
Which showed me that
you had swapped the heat tape.

396
00:24:13,787 --> 00:24:16,039
Something that you could've
been sent out to clean for.

397
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:21,128
And you wouldn't do that to anyone,
you would do that to someone that you...

398
00:24:23,505 --> 00:24:25,090
Yeah, well, that was then.

399
00:24:26,466 --> 00:24:29,803
So what? You don't remember something,
poof, it's gone.

400
00:24:30,888 --> 00:24:32,681
Might just as well not have existed.

401
00:24:35,184 --> 00:24:36,185
Okay.

402
00:24:38,353 --> 00:24:39,354
Well...

403
00:24:42,024 --> 00:24:43,275
The note I got said,

404
00:24:44,067 --> 00:24:47,446
"If you want to know the truth,
go to the marketplace at 2:00."

405
00:24:48,530 --> 00:24:50,866
So I wanna know,
do I go to the marketplace?

406
00:24:51,491 --> 00:24:52,868
Do I go... [stammers]

407
00:24:53,619 --> 00:24:54,620
[sighs]

408
00:24:56,163 --> 00:24:58,999
You know what was
the most irritating thing about you?

409
00:25:00,417 --> 00:25:04,420
If there was a problem,
a question, a decision to be made

410
00:25:04,421 --> 00:25:07,048
and everyone was pointing
in one direction,

411
00:25:07,049 --> 00:25:10,551
you would decide that the only direction
to go in was the opposite.

412
00:25:10,552 --> 00:25:12,929
Right. Okay. That's quite irritating.

413
00:25:12,930 --> 00:25:16,934
No. What was irritating
was that 99% of the time you were right.

414
00:25:18,769 --> 00:25:20,687
Because you trusted your gut.

415
00:25:22,981 --> 00:25:25,817
So my question to you now is,
what does your gut say?

416
00:25:32,115 --> 00:25:33,533
I can't.

417
00:25:33,534 --> 00:25:38,204
[smacks lips] You see, the person I risked
my life for, she was a fighter.

418
00:25:38,205 --> 00:25:41,499
She would have fought
with every inch of stubbornness,

419
00:25:41,500 --> 00:25:44,002
arrogance, pigheadedness in her body.

420
00:25:49,716 --> 00:25:51,969
[stammers] That's it?

421
00:25:59,351 --> 00:26:01,311
- What's that?
- Are you even trying?

422
00:26:02,145 --> 00:26:03,230
To remember?

423
00:26:04,898 --> 00:26:08,402
What do you m... I'm here. Right?
So, yeah. What...

424
00:26:10,028 --> 00:26:11,154
I get...

425
00:26:12,447 --> 00:26:16,701
I get splinters of memory, or something.
I don't know what it is.

426
00:26:16,702 --> 00:26:21,790
And the-the more I try and hold onto it,
it just slips away so...

427
00:26:22,791 --> 00:26:23,834
I don't know what to do.

428
00:26:25,711 --> 00:26:26,712
Try harder.

429
00:26:36,513 --> 00:26:37,806
I don't remember you.

430
00:26:39,016 --> 00:26:42,269
Ah, that's okay.
I'm not taking it personally.

431
00:26:43,520 --> 00:26:44,979
You're the only one.

432
00:26:44,980 --> 00:26:46,356
How about we go sit over there?

433
00:26:46,982 --> 00:26:51,320
It's so good to get away
from the constant staring, and questions...

434
00:26:53,155 --> 00:26:57,242
and pressure to vomit up
something of value.

435
00:26:58,911 --> 00:27:04,333
You have any military visitors yet,
hoping you'll vomit up something of value?

436
00:27:05,459 --> 00:27:06,543
Not yet.

437
00:27:08,921 --> 00:27:13,133
Mostly... [sighs]
...my brother hovering over me,

438
00:27:13,717 --> 00:27:16,469
sweating bullets
that I'll remember something.

439
00:27:16,470 --> 00:27:18,555
- Yeah.
- Remember him.

440
00:27:19,765 --> 00:27:23,519
Well, for the record, I'm a reporter.

441
00:27:24,895 --> 00:27:26,647
I want you to vomit up something too.

442
00:27:29,775 --> 00:27:31,026
We met a couple of times.

443
00:27:31,527 --> 00:27:33,819
Something was bothering you
about the mission to Iran.

444
00:27:33,820 --> 00:27:35,696
I don't remember anything
about the mission.

445
00:27:35,697 --> 00:27:39,116
Part of what I do is help people
get their stories straight, okay?

446
00:27:39,117 --> 00:27:40,701
I help them fill in the blanks.

447
00:27:40,702 --> 00:27:44,705
So I thought I could tell you what I know
about the mission

448
00:27:44,706 --> 00:27:47,458
and what they're saying officially.

449
00:27:47,459 --> 00:27:49,795
Maybe that'll help stir something up.

450
00:28:00,097 --> 00:28:01,848
[Charlotte panting]

451
00:28:01,849 --> 00:28:02,932
Charlotte.

452
00:28:02,933 --> 00:28:05,560
[thunder rumbling]

453
00:28:05,561 --> 00:28:06,645
[distorted] Charlotte?

454
00:28:07,729 --> 00:28:10,732
- Hey. Hey. Are you okay? Hey.
- [panting, breathes shakily]

455
00:28:13,068 --> 00:28:14,820
[breathing shakily]

456
00:28:32,129 --> 00:28:33,380
- Are you okay?
- Yeah.

457
00:28:39,219 --> 00:28:42,598
All right, excuse me. Hey,
excuse me, guys. Thank you. Excuse me.

458
00:28:45,142 --> 00:28:46,602
[people chattering]

459
00:28:59,823 --> 00:29:03,034
Look, I, uh... I know
this is not a part of your orders,

460
00:29:03,035 --> 00:29:04,660
- but I-I need some space.
- Mm-hmm.

461
00:29:04,661 --> 00:29:07,914
So, um, could you give me 30 minutes or...

462
00:29:07,915 --> 00:29:09,625
Have you met Mrs. Sims?

463
00:29:10,209 --> 00:29:13,169
I let you outta my sight, then she'll use
my intestines for dental floss--

464
00:29:13,170 --> 00:29:14,755
- I know, but I need to--
- Juliette.

465
00:29:15,255 --> 00:29:17,048
- It's her. It's Juliette.
- [Jerry] Hi, guys.

466
00:29:17,049 --> 00:29:19,383
- Wait, wait. Don't.
- Hey, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

467
00:29:19,384 --> 00:29:22,053
- All right, guys. Sorry, no.
- [Juliette] Jerry. Hey. No, no, no.

468
00:29:22,054 --> 00:29:23,596
Just give the mayor some space, okay?

469
00:29:23,597 --> 00:29:25,223
- Back up.
- What the fuck are you...

470
00:29:25,224 --> 00:29:26,974
- Did you touch me?
- What are you talking about?

471
00:29:26,975 --> 00:29:28,351
- He fucking touched me!
- [clamoring]

472
00:29:28,352 --> 00:29:29,644
- I'm sorry.
- Hey!

473
00:29:29,645 --> 00:29:32,230
[all clamoring]

474
00:29:32,231 --> 00:29:34,857
- I'm sorry.
- You need to take your hands off her.

475
00:29:34,858 --> 00:29:37,986
Madam Mayor. This is a misunderstanding.

476
00:29:39,404 --> 00:29:41,615
[shouting, grunting]

477
00:30:01,051 --> 00:30:02,553
Ma'am. Are you lost?

478
00:30:03,887 --> 00:30:06,557
If you want the truth, come with me.

479
00:30:09,935 --> 00:30:11,061
[sighs]

480
00:30:12,980 --> 00:30:13,980
[pants]

481
00:30:13,981 --> 00:30:15,815
Match me. Three...

482
00:30:15,816 --> 00:30:17,900
[exhales, inhales deeply]

483
00:30:17,901 --> 00:30:19,777
That's it. [blows]

484
00:30:19,778 --> 00:30:22,613
- [breathes deeply]
- That's it. Match my breathing.

485
00:30:22,614 --> 00:30:24,908
- In for three seconds and hold.
- [pants] Okay.

486
00:30:26,159 --> 00:30:28,286
And out. [exhales deeply]

487
00:30:28,287 --> 00:30:30,580
Good. You're doing good. Easy now.

488
00:30:30,581 --> 00:30:33,250
- Let it go. Okay. Good.
- [breathes deeply]

489
00:30:34,543 --> 00:30:35,877
[Victor] <i>Charlotte seemed to know her.</i>

490
00:30:35,878 --> 00:30:38,172
- Did you call the police?
- No, I was waiting for you.

491
00:30:58,817 --> 00:31:00,652
[keys rattle]

492
00:31:08,619 --> 00:31:10,203
- Who... Who are you?
- [keys rattling]

493
00:31:10,204 --> 00:31:13,289
- [lock clicks]
- [chuckles]

494
00:31:13,290 --> 00:31:14,582
What?

495
00:31:14,583 --> 00:31:15,667
[chuckles]

496
00:31:17,294 --> 00:31:21,797
Um... I-I'm the man you strong-armed
into helping you with the hard drive.

497
00:31:21,798 --> 00:31:23,382
- What?
- Uh...

498
00:31:23,383 --> 00:31:26,010
Patrick Kennedy, whose life's
never sucked more than when I met you.

499
00:31:26,011 --> 00:31:27,386
You're one of the fugitives.

500
00:31:27,387 --> 00:31:29,931
Uh, Danny. You screwed my life too.

501
00:31:29,932 --> 00:31:33,352
Landed me in Judicial seclusion.
Trying to forget it, probably never will.

502
00:31:33,936 --> 00:31:36,687
Sandy. I worked for you
when you were sheriff.

503
00:31:36,688 --> 00:31:39,483
You didn't screw up my life
but, uh, I still don't like you.

504
00:31:40,192 --> 00:31:42,193
Okay, and you... [stammers]
You sent me the note?

505
00:31:42,194 --> 00:31:44,904
[stammers] Well, I didn't. She did.
She wrote it. I don't know why she wrote

506
00:31:44,905 --> 00:31:46,989
- the whole thing about the truth.
- You sent me the note?

507
00:31:46,990 --> 00:31:48,491
- 'Cause I wanted her to show up.
- Yeah.

508
00:31:48,492 --> 00:31:49,951
Okay, but what you should have said is,

509
00:31:49,952 --> 00:31:52,287
- "Why are you lying about the outside?"
- [Sandy] Yeah.

510
00:31:52,829 --> 00:31:54,664
Well, I'm-I'm not lying. What do you mean?

511
00:31:54,665 --> 00:31:57,917
The Jane Carmody video. I saw you see it.

512
00:31:57,918 --> 00:32:00,086
- [stammers]
- The flowers. And the trees.

513
00:32:00,087 --> 00:32:01,922
- And the birds.
- And the birds.

514
00:32:02,506 --> 00:32:04,465
And then you went outside
and you came back...

515
00:32:04,466 --> 00:32:05,925
- [grunts]
- ...right?

516
00:32:05,926 --> 00:32:07,885
So why are you saying that it's not safe?

517
00:32:07,886 --> 00:32:11,180
It's not safe 'cause it's not safe.

518
00:32:11,181 --> 00:32:14,976
When you went over the hill,
what did you see?

519
00:32:14,977 --> 00:32:16,978
I've seen what... what we've all seen.

520
00:32:16,979 --> 00:32:18,062
I've seen the... [stammers]

521
00:32:18,063 --> 00:32:21,440
I walk over the hill
and then I walk into a refuge hut, right?

522
00:32:21,441 --> 00:32:22,900
But do you remember that?

523
00:32:22,901 --> 00:32:24,069
- Really?
- Huh?

524
00:32:24,570 --> 00:32:27,155
Or are you just remembering
what you've been shown?

525
00:32:31,368 --> 00:32:32,578
No, I...

526
00:32:35,247 --> 00:32:36,999
I... Okay. I don't...

527
00:32:38,166 --> 00:32:39,626
I don't. I don't know. I don't know.

528
00:32:40,210 --> 00:32:41,503
I don't know. I don't know.

529
00:32:42,254 --> 00:32:45,214
Then... [stammers] ...maybe they did it.
Maybe... Maybe they actually did it.

530
00:32:45,215 --> 00:32:46,883
- What?
- [stammers] Hold on one second.

531
00:32:46,884 --> 00:32:49,677
You remember the, um...
the drugs that Sims offered me?

532
00:32:49,678 --> 00:32:52,346
"Swallow these pills and you'll forget
that we led your wife to her grave"?

533
00:32:52,347 --> 00:32:54,056
Do you remember that?
I said it was total bullshit.

534
00:32:54,057 --> 00:32:55,224
- Hey.
- Maybe it's not bullshit.

535
00:32:55,225 --> 00:32:57,311
- I'm sorry. All I'm saying is...
- Just...

536
00:32:58,353 --> 00:33:01,481
What if your head injury
is not because of the fire?

537
00:33:02,441 --> 00:33:03,441
Let me ask you something.

538
00:33:03,442 --> 00:33:05,735
Are-Are you receiving
a lot of medical attention right now?

539
00:33:05,736 --> 00:33:07,278
- Yeah.
- Yeah, like being asked to swallow

540
00:33:07,279 --> 00:33:08,530
a whole bunch of pills?

541
00:33:10,282 --> 00:33:11,491
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

542
00:33:13,660 --> 00:33:15,703
[smacks lips] Wake the fuck up, people.
I'm sorry.

543
00:33:15,704 --> 00:33:20,791
But you are the only one
who has ever gone out and survived.

544
00:33:20,792 --> 00:33:24,295
And now, surprise, sur-fucking-prise.

545
00:33:24,296 --> 00:33:26,507
She doesn't remember anything
except what they tell her.

546
00:33:29,384 --> 00:33:30,719
Get the helmets.

547
00:33:31,595 --> 00:33:32,971
[sighs, mutters]

548
00:33:35,349 --> 00:33:36,350
[sighs]

549
00:33:39,186 --> 00:33:42,105
Hey. Hey. What the fuck were you doing?

550
00:33:42,940 --> 00:33:44,315
What she wanted.

551
00:33:44,316 --> 00:33:46,275
- [scoffs]
- Our non-date?

552
00:33:46,276 --> 00:33:48,945
- Wasn't randomly fishing for information.
- Oh, I know.

553
00:33:48,946 --> 00:33:51,364
You went to Charlotte for a story,
and then when you didn't get it,

554
00:33:51,365 --> 00:33:53,867
- you came to me.
- No, you idiot, I didn't go to her.

555
00:33:54,826 --> 00:33:56,078
She came to me.

556
00:33:56,662 --> 00:33:57,663
[sighs]

557
00:33:58,705 --> 00:33:59,706
She read my stuff.

558
00:34:00,207 --> 00:34:02,292
She thought she could trust me
and we met in secret.

559
00:34:03,752 --> 00:34:07,213
There were questions that she wanted
to ask, questions that she couldn't ask.

560
00:34:07,214 --> 00:34:09,757
The best she could do
was point me in the right direction.

561
00:34:16,139 --> 00:34:17,431
Right direction for what?

562
00:34:18,559 --> 00:34:20,811
She thought something was off
about the mission to Iran.

563
00:34:21,812 --> 00:34:24,814
She thought you might be willing to use
what little congressional power you've got

564
00:34:24,815 --> 00:34:26,315
to try and find some answers.

565
00:34:28,150 --> 00:34:29,319
What kind of questions?

566
00:34:30,737 --> 00:34:33,823
The kind that an administration
with a hard-on for going to war with Iran

567
00:34:33,824 --> 00:34:34,949
don't want asked.

568
00:34:35,449 --> 00:34:38,078
Like, "Was the dirty bomb attack
on D.C. even real?"

569
00:34:41,873 --> 00:34:45,502
She told me not all missions planned
for Iran were related to the nukes.

570
00:34:46,210 --> 00:34:49,129
The one she was on, going to the mountains
up next to Turkmenistan,

571
00:34:49,130 --> 00:34:51,884
their planes were going to be loaded
with bunker busters.

572
00:34:52,801 --> 00:34:55,011
She said they-they pulled out
the comms units

573
00:34:55,012 --> 00:34:57,180
and installed this old school system
from the '80s.

574
00:34:57,181 --> 00:34:58,515
Why would they do that?

575
00:34:59,433 --> 00:35:01,100
What do old comms give you? [sighs]

576
00:35:01,101 --> 00:35:03,978
[stammers] You don't want
your email hacked? You use a typewriter.

577
00:35:03,979 --> 00:35:06,856
Don't want your planes hacked? You take
them out of the digital playing field.

578
00:35:06,857 --> 00:35:09,234
She said,
"Might as well be flying Tomcats."

579
00:35:11,570 --> 00:35:13,279
Everyone else
in Charlotte's squadron is dead,

580
00:35:13,280 --> 00:35:15,490
and she scores a magical transfer here?

581
00:35:16,283 --> 00:35:17,659
Where her treatment will what?

582
00:35:18,285 --> 00:35:19,953
Make sure she never remembers
what happened?

583
00:35:22,998 --> 00:35:25,709
I didn't sneak in there today
to get some story.

584
00:35:27,085 --> 00:35:28,504
Your sister wanted the truth.

585
00:35:29,922 --> 00:35:32,966
Badly enough to risk her career
and risk going to jail for it.

586
00:35:34,301 --> 00:35:35,302
I think she still does.

587
00:35:36,470 --> 00:35:41,058
Which just leaves the question,
was she right to send me to you for help?

588
00:35:44,478 --> 00:35:46,897
And then a raider came up behind her.

589
00:35:48,357 --> 00:35:53,195
They talked for a few seconds
and then Juliette followed the raider.

590
00:35:54,821 --> 00:35:57,032
Down an alley
and into one of the storage rooms.

591
00:36:00,244 --> 00:36:01,703
Which storage room?

592
00:36:06,542 --> 00:36:07,751
I can show you...

593
00:36:09,878 --> 00:36:11,380
but there's something I want in return.

594
00:36:12,089 --> 00:36:13,715
And what might that be?

595
00:36:19,721 --> 00:36:22,808
This one we grabbed from IT the other day.

596
00:36:23,308 --> 00:36:24,977
Printed inside is the number 18.

597
00:36:25,978 --> 00:36:27,521
Same number as on the hard drive.

598
00:36:28,730 --> 00:36:31,441
But this one, the helmet you wore back?

599
00:36:33,026 --> 00:36:34,152
It says 17.

600
00:36:51,336 --> 00:36:52,337
[Sandy] Juliette.

601
00:36:53,672 --> 00:36:54,673
You had a sign.

602
00:36:55,507 --> 00:37:00,804
You held it up to the camera
and it said, "Not safe. Do not come out."

603
00:37:04,725 --> 00:37:06,351
I worked for you.

604
00:37:07,102 --> 00:37:09,104
I know your handwriting.
You didn't write it.

605
00:37:10,564 --> 00:37:11,814
Who wrote the sign?

606
00:37:11,815 --> 00:37:14,693
I don't know. I don't know.
I don't know... It's fucking gone.

607
00:37:27,539 --> 00:37:28,707
[whistles]

608
00:37:30,334 --> 00:37:31,835
[whistles]

609
00:37:34,171 --> 00:37:35,464
Great. What now?

610
00:37:36,173 --> 00:37:38,049
We get back to looking for Lukas Kyle.

611
00:37:38,050 --> 00:37:39,550
Ninety-two days without a sighting.

612
00:37:39,551 --> 00:37:41,427
But you know what they say,
93rd day's a charm.

613
00:37:41,428 --> 00:37:43,095
Hey. Hey, wait, Lukas Kyle?

614
00:37:43,096 --> 00:37:45,348
- [Sandy] Yeah, he was Bernard's shadow.
- No, I know. I know.

615
00:37:45,349 --> 00:37:46,516
Why are you looking for him?

616
00:37:46,517 --> 00:37:49,352
Because before he became Bernard's shadow,
he was in the mines.

617
00:37:49,353 --> 00:37:50,436
Why?

618
00:37:50,437 --> 00:37:53,815
Because you showed him
a red-level relic and he didn't report it.

619
00:37:54,316 --> 00:37:56,025
I figured you showed him that hard drive.

620
00:37:56,026 --> 00:37:57,110
I don't...

621
00:37:58,820 --> 00:38:00,279
I don't remember giving him...

622
00:38:00,280 --> 00:38:02,615
[stammers] I know.
You don't remember anything. I got it.

623
00:38:02,616 --> 00:38:03,699
Wa-wa-wait. Stop.

624
00:38:03,700 --> 00:38:07,370
So Lukas is sent to the mines,
but why then did Bernard pull him out?

625
00:38:07,371 --> 00:38:08,663
Why? I don't get it.

626
00:38:08,664 --> 00:38:10,498
That's what we're trying to find out.

627
00:38:10,499 --> 00:38:12,542
But he hasn't been seen
since the rebellion.

628
00:38:12,543 --> 00:38:14,001
[whistles in distance]

629
00:38:14,002 --> 00:38:16,212
Fuck. Get her out of here. Go, go, go, go.

630
00:38:16,213 --> 00:38:17,505
- You gotta go. Now.
- We gotta go.

631
00:38:17,506 --> 00:38:18,590
- Now.
- Hey. Okay.

632
00:38:24,221 --> 00:38:26,431
Madam Mayor, step aside please.

633
00:38:28,600 --> 00:38:30,059
- Bring in the ram.
- Are you all right?

634
00:38:30,060 --> 00:38:31,144
- Yeah.
- Hold on.

635
00:38:31,937 --> 00:38:33,480
Clear the mayor! Get her out.

636
00:38:35,649 --> 00:38:36,899
[Emerson] Now!

637
00:38:36,900 --> 00:38:38,068
[grunts]

638
00:38:45,784 --> 00:38:46,785
[sighs]

639
00:39:21,904 --> 00:39:23,071
What did Kennedy want?

640
00:39:24,364 --> 00:39:26,782
He just kept on going on
about it being safe outside,

641
00:39:26,783 --> 00:39:29,410
that I was lying
and I need to tell the truth.

642
00:39:29,411 --> 00:39:32,372
- But you know the truth.
- Yeah. I do.

643
00:39:36,502 --> 00:39:39,212
When you had the dream about Bernard,

644
00:39:39,213 --> 00:39:43,466
you said there was something
you had to do but couldn't remember what.

645
00:39:43,467 --> 00:39:44,551
Yeah?

646
00:39:45,093 --> 00:39:47,011
Did Kennedy say anything about that?

647
00:39:47,012 --> 00:39:48,096
What? No.

648
00:39:48,931 --> 00:39:53,268
No, he just kept yelling that I need
to tell everyone that it's good outside.

649
00:39:54,228 --> 00:39:55,604
- That's all he said?
- Yeah.

650
00:39:56,104 --> 00:39:57,105
Okay.

651
00:39:59,399 --> 00:40:02,527
Look, you get that because of that note,

652
00:40:02,528 --> 00:40:05,446
you walked blindly into something
that could've gone very wrong?

653
00:40:05,447 --> 00:40:09,116
- Yeah. [clears throat]
- This is why I have Jerry on you. Okay?

654
00:40:09,117 --> 00:40:10,701
Because the only way I can keep you safe

655
00:40:10,702 --> 00:40:12,246
- is if we're in it together.
- Okay.

656
00:40:13,080 --> 00:40:14,540
There can't be secrets.

657
00:40:16,083 --> 00:40:19,710
I know. And I didn't think it through
and I'm sorry.

658
00:40:19,711 --> 00:40:21,421
I should... I shouldn't have. I know.

659
00:40:22,297 --> 00:40:24,048
- I know.
- Okay.

660
00:40:24,049 --> 00:40:25,466
- Okay?
- Yeah.

661
00:40:25,467 --> 00:40:28,845
- Yeah. Are we good?
- Not quite. I, um...

662
00:40:28,846 --> 00:40:30,305
I need you to meet someone.

663
00:40:32,766 --> 00:40:34,058
Why?

664
00:40:34,059 --> 00:40:36,811
She followed you. [sighs] She...

665
00:40:36,812 --> 00:40:39,397
The only way she'd tell us where you were
is if she could talk to you.

666
00:40:39,398 --> 00:40:41,983
- I said she could have one minute.
- Are you kidding me? I'm tired.

667
00:40:41,984 --> 00:40:43,402
- It's a minute.
- [sighs]

668
00:40:43,986 --> 00:40:45,904
- Okay. Yep. Mm-hmm.
- Yeah?

669
00:40:51,577 --> 00:40:53,411
This is, um...

670
00:40:53,412 --> 00:40:54,788
Juliette.

671
00:40:55,831 --> 00:40:59,126
- Right.
- We call ourselves that. In tribute.

672
00:41:03,505 --> 00:41:04,506
Oh.

673
00:41:05,174 --> 00:41:06,716
Did it hurt getting that?

674
00:41:06,717 --> 00:41:08,385
[chuckles] A bit.

675
00:41:10,262 --> 00:41:11,387
[chuckles] A lot, actually.

676
00:41:11,388 --> 00:41:12,973
You know what it means?

677
00:41:14,975 --> 00:41:16,559
- No.
- [Juliette] No?

678
00:41:16,560 --> 00:41:20,772
We just... We just thought that if you
had it then it must mean something good.

679
00:41:21,273 --> 00:41:22,274
Right.

680
00:41:22,774 --> 00:41:24,693
[fan] Would you tell me what it means?

681
00:41:25,986 --> 00:41:29,406
Sorry, what's your name? What do
your parents... What do they call you?

682
00:41:30,908 --> 00:41:32,408
- Evelyn.
- Evelyn.

683
00:41:32,409 --> 00:41:33,660
Well, that's a good name.

684
00:41:35,287 --> 00:41:36,288
Um...

685
00:41:41,376 --> 00:41:43,085
I don't know what it means.

686
00:41:43,086 --> 00:41:44,545
I don't even remember getting it.

687
00:41:44,546 --> 00:41:49,009
So, I guess you just got marks
on your body that don't mean anything.

688
00:41:51,512 --> 00:41:53,180
But, yeah.

689
00:41:53,764 --> 00:41:54,765
Sorry.

690
00:41:56,099 --> 00:41:58,601
- [Evelyn] Maybe.
- Hmm?

691
00:41:58,602 --> 00:41:59,686
[Evelyn] Maybe not.

692
00:42:00,187 --> 00:42:01,355
You went out.

693
00:42:01,939 --> 00:42:07,402
And you came back
to keep us from killing ourselves.

694
00:42:10,614 --> 00:42:13,242
So if these lines mean
nothing else to you,

695
00:42:14,910 --> 00:42:16,370
well, they mean that to us.

696
00:42:29,258 --> 00:42:32,886
- Let's not have that day again. Okay?
- Yeah.

697
00:42:38,225 --> 00:42:40,310
There are two things
I want to say to you. Hey!

698
00:42:42,604 --> 00:42:45,774
Thing one: That stunt in the market?

699
00:42:46,692 --> 00:42:47,860
Not cool.

700
00:42:48,569 --> 00:42:51,488
I am not the kind of man
who would ever lay hands on a woman.

701
00:42:52,239 --> 00:42:55,742
I honor this badge and my wife.

702
00:42:56,451 --> 00:43:01,081
Yeah. That was a shitty thing to do.
And I'm just not myself right now.

703
00:43:01,707 --> 00:43:02,708
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

704
00:43:03,500 --> 00:43:09,631
Thing two: I've been thinking about
this whole mayoral security thing.

705
00:43:11,341 --> 00:43:13,677
It basically feels like we're treating you
like a prisoner.

706
00:43:15,762 --> 00:43:17,347
And it's not sitting right with me.

707
00:43:19,725 --> 00:43:22,102
Okay, if you wanna get away from all this...

708
00:43:24,688 --> 00:43:27,649
take some time for yourself,
you can tell me.

709
00:43:29,651 --> 00:43:32,571
- I-I'll find a way to make it work.
- What about Camille?

710
00:43:37,159 --> 00:43:38,160
You're my mayor.

711
00:43:39,953 --> 00:43:41,830
You let me worry about Mrs. Sims.

712
00:43:44,958 --> 00:43:46,126
Come on.

713
00:44:12,277 --> 00:44:14,904
You might wanna wear long sleeves
till that heals.

714
00:44:14,905 --> 00:44:17,491
They're looking for a woman
with a damaged right wrist.

715
00:44:20,202 --> 00:44:22,704
I've looked the other way for weeks, Kat.

716
00:44:23,664 --> 00:44:24,789
I haven't put my foot down,

717
00:44:24,790 --> 00:44:26,457
- associating with Kennedy--
- Your foot down?

718
00:44:26,458 --> 00:44:29,461
- You're really gonna go there.
- I don't know where the fuck I'm going.

719
00:44:30,462 --> 00:44:31,629
You saw the page.

720
00:44:31,630 --> 00:44:33,464
The world was beautiful.

721
00:44:33,465 --> 00:44:35,175
You think you need to remind me?

722
00:44:36,802 --> 00:44:40,763
I've spent my whole life
living by something,

723
00:44:40,764 --> 00:44:42,849
enforcing something which is...

724
00:44:42,850 --> 00:44:44,268
- A lie.
- No.

725
00:44:45,811 --> 00:44:46,937
Not all of it.

726
00:44:48,897 --> 00:44:50,481
But if you think there's not a single day

727
00:44:50,482 --> 00:44:54,194
I don't choke on the irony
of being asked to help rewrite the Pact...

728
00:44:56,029 --> 00:44:59,867
How many times do I see the things
that I believed in are wrong?

729
00:45:00,450 --> 00:45:02,035
But what you're doing...

730
00:45:03,996 --> 00:45:08,292
I can't tell you or Kennedy
or anyone else what to think.

731
00:45:08,959 --> 00:45:11,919
But today, things went too far.

732
00:45:11,920 --> 00:45:16,716
Think what you want!
Don't break into IT! Don't grab the mayor!

733
00:45:16,717 --> 00:45:18,427
Your daughter is sleeping.

734
00:45:25,767 --> 00:45:28,437
It's a miracle no one else saw you.

735
00:45:30,063 --> 00:45:33,649
What if they had? What happens then?

736
00:45:33,650 --> 00:45:37,362
When they tell me
my wife is in the middle of this.

737
00:45:38,197 --> 00:45:39,656
I don't know, Paul.

738
00:45:40,240 --> 00:45:42,618
I'm struggling to get a bead
on things too.

739
00:45:45,287 --> 00:45:46,288
[computer beeps]

740
00:46:06,558 --> 00:46:07,726
I gotta go.

741
00:46:09,228 --> 00:46:10,229
Where?

742
00:46:11,772 --> 00:46:12,773
The Mids.

743
00:46:13,982 --> 00:46:15,275
There's a missing person.

744
00:46:17,528 --> 00:46:21,532
[door opens, closes]

745
00:46:25,619 --> 00:46:26,662
Blood pressure's up.

746
00:46:27,913 --> 00:46:29,081
[sighs]

747
00:46:30,165 --> 00:46:33,001
Not surprising
after such a frightening day, right?

748
00:46:33,752 --> 00:46:35,045
Yeah.

749
00:46:35,671 --> 00:46:37,965
- Time for your vitamins.
- Thanks.

750
00:46:50,519 --> 00:46:52,521
- All right. Bye.
- Bye.

751
00:47:09,454 --> 00:47:10,455
[spits]

752
00:47:13,250 --> 00:47:17,087
Hey. Um, I'm gonna go to the cafeteria.
I'm, uh... I'm hungry.

753
00:47:17,588 --> 00:47:18,672
[clears throat]

754
00:47:31,185 --> 00:47:32,561
[sighs]

755
00:47:51,371 --> 00:47:52,831
[Juliette] <i>Never noticed that before.</i>

756
00:47:53,498 --> 00:47:54,958
Yeah, well, now you have.

757
00:47:59,213 --> 00:48:01,089
Ma'am, would you like to order something?

758
00:48:02,174 --> 00:48:04,384
No. I'm good.

759
00:49:22,921 --> 00:49:25,465
On the upside,
I think it gave her a scare.

760
00:49:26,175 --> 00:49:29,302
She apologized for not telling me
about the note straightaway.

761
00:49:29,303 --> 00:49:31,638
[The Algorithm] <i>So you believe
things are back on track.</i>

762
00:49:32,598 --> 00:49:35,683
I can't vouch for tomorrow,
but today, yes.

763
00:49:35,684 --> 00:49:36,768
I think they are.

764
00:49:39,813 --> 00:49:43,901
[The Algorithm] <i>After her return,
Juliette's survival was unwelcome.</i>

765
00:49:44,568 --> 00:49:46,653
<i>But the numbers indicated
it was tolerable.</i>

766
00:49:47,529 --> 00:49:49,031
<i>Perhaps even beneficial.</i>

767
00:49:50,365 --> 00:49:53,451
<i>The blue line represents the calming value
in the Silo</i>

768
00:49:53,452 --> 00:49:55,495
<i>of having Juliette Nichols as mayor.</i>

769
00:49:56,496 --> 00:50:01,418
<i>The red line is the risk she poses
if the memory protocol is not successful.</i>

770
00:50:02,628 --> 00:50:07,508
<i>If those lines cross, Juliette Nichols
will have outlived her usefulness.</i>

771
00:50:08,800 --> 00:50:12,345
<i>The problem, of course,
is that a sudden removal of Juliette</i>

772
00:50:12,346 --> 00:50:15,807
<i>could be catastrophically destabilizing.</i>

773
00:50:17,100 --> 00:50:20,561
<i>We can only hope that the introduction
of the vitamin compound</i>

774
00:50:20,562 --> 00:50:24,191
<i>into the water supply happens
before that becomes necessary.</i>

775
00:50:25,859 --> 00:50:28,111
[metal clanking]

